  "slug": "anisotropic-cribrosa-036e",
  "summary": "Three recent items apply mechanistic simulation to cancer at vaccine, cell-migration and membrane scales, spanning PBPK, hybrid FSI and molecular dynamics.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational modeling of cancer systems in which explicit physical or physiological parameters are linked to a cell-scale or tissue-scale outcome. The migration study couples a dissipative particle dynamics solvent to an immersed boundary representation of the cell, so that cell deformation enters the analysis of lateral migration in microchannel flow. The membrane study uses molecular dynamics of prostate cell membranes built from cell-line lipidomes to resolve leaflet-specific fluidity and disease-state-dependent phase behavior. The vaccination study is a multiscale PBPK model that connects lymphatic transport, antigen-presenting cell activation and immune priming to vaccine timing. What is new is the explicit coupling of measured or derived structural detail to a predicted behavior within each system. The shared element is methodological rather than a common mechanism, since the vaccine model is not mechanical and the membrane work is not a tissue-level study.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-23"
}
